EXT. DRY GOAT PEN BEHIND A FORSAKEN CHURCH - EVENING

1878. Dust. A broken fence. A leaning cross. Three goats chew at nothing.

SHERIFF AUGUSTINE TREVINO, 60s, tin star pinned crooked, stands over a shallow grave. Hat in his hands. ABIGAIL FROST-MWANGI, 30s, widow's black, holds a rusted shovel. Between them, a boy, JEREMIAH FROST, 10, squats by a goat and strokes its ear.

The wind pushes a tumbleweed through the pen. Nobody watches it go.

AUGUSTINE
Ma'am.

Abigail doesn't turn. She sets the shovel against the fence. Dusts her palms on her skirt. One at a time.

AUGUSTINE (CONT'D)
He'd have wanted a preacher.

ABIGAIL
He'd have wanted his horse.

Augustine nods. Looks at the cross. Looks at the boy. Jeremiah lifts a handful of dry grass for the goat.

AUGUSTINE
I'll see to the horse.

ABIGAIL
Don't trouble yourself.

AUGUSTINE
No trouble.

She takes the hat from his hands and sets it on the cross. The brim catches the wind. She weights it with a stone.

ABIGAIL
That's his now.

Augustine opens his mouth. Closes it. Pulls a folded paper from his vest and holds it out. She doesn't take it.

ABIGAIL (CONT'D)
Read it to him.

Augustine looks at the grave. Clears his throat. Doesn't read. He folds the paper smaller, crouches, and tucks it into the dirt beside the cross.

Jeremiah stands, brushes his knees. Walks to his mother. Takes her hand. Together they look at the cross until the light turns orange. The goats wander off.
